          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have both asthma and reflux and my cough is worse at night. Which is more likely to be driving it, and what does guidance describe for each?",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine.** No emergency pattern matched, but that is not a clearance. If you develop breathlessness at rest, chest pain, or a cough that gets worse, seek medical advice promptly. Call emergency services if you cannot breathe comfortably. The reflux guidance also lists seeking urgent help if your breathing is getting worse, you are struggling to speak or breathe, your lips or face look blue, or your cough brings up blood. Seek prompt review for difficulty or pain when swallowing, unplanned weight loss, vomiting blood, black stools, or persistent vomiting, which are reflux alarm features in the guidance.\n\nThe sources I could retrieve cannot tell which condition is driving your night-time cough, and they do not say how to weigh the two when someone has both. A clinician who knows your history and can examine you is the right person to work that out.\n\n**Asthma.** Night-time or early-morning cough is a typical asthma pattern, and cough alone can be how asthma presents. Asthma is diagnosed from a matching symptom pattern plus objective evidence of variable airflow limitation, usually spirometry before and after a bronchodilator. A normal test on a good day does not rule it out. The guidance says that a short-acting reliever alone is no longer appropriate at any severity, and that adults and adolescents should receive inhaled-corticosteroid-containing treatment.\n\n**Reflux.** Reflux is usually treated on a trial basis in adults with typical symptoms and no alarm features. The guidance describes an eight-week trial of acid suppression, taken before a meal, without endoscopy first. Lifestyle measures include raising the head of the bed for night-time symptoms and avoiding meals within two to three hours of lying down. Weight reduction has the strongest supporting evidence of the lifestyle measures. Chronic cough is a less typical reflux presentation. The sources say these symptoms are less specific and respond less predictably to acid suppression, so attributing a cough to reflux without considering other causes is unreliable.\n\n**Where sources differ.** For mild asthma, global strategy guidance describes an inhaled-corticosteroid-and-formoterol combination as the preferred reliever across steps. Some national panel guidance says a short-acting bronchodilator reliever alongside a regular controller remains appropriate in mild disease. Both positions are real, and which one applies depends on the guidance your clinician follows.\n\n**Gaps.** I could not retrieve guidance on reflux-related cough, including how to test for it or when to treat it. Nothing I retrieved grades the strength of evidence for either cause of night-time cough, and I could not find further red-flag detail for chronic cough.\n\nA useful step is to bring a short diary to your clinician: when the cough starts, whether it relates to meals or lying down, whether it comes with wheeze or chest tightness, and whether your asthma medicines help it. Please discuss your treatment plan with the clinician who manages your asthma and reflux.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
